WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:03.680
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:08.820
In this series, we talk about research on
all kinds of physics simulations, including

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:14.400
fluids, collision physics, and we have even
ventured into hair simulations.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:18.980
If you look here at this beautiful footage,
you may be surprised to know how many moving

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:22.660
parts a researcher has to get right to get
something like this.

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:27.860
For instance, some of these simulations have
to go down to the level of computing the physics

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:30.240
between individual hair strands.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:34.809
If it is done well, like what you see here
from our earlier episode, these simulations

00:00:34.809 --> 00:00:40.170
will properly show us how things should move,
but that’s not all - there is also an abundance

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:43.289
of research works out there on how they should
look.

00:00:43.289 --> 00:00:49.399
And even then, we’re not done, because before
that, we have to take a step back and somehow

00:00:49.399 --> 00:00:55.930
create these digital 3D models that show us
the geometry of these flamboyant hairstyles.

00:00:55.930 --> 00:01:01.280
Approximately 300 episodes ago, we talked
about a technique that took a photograph as

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:07.150
an input, and created a digital 3D model that
we can use in our simulations and rendering

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:08.150
systems.

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:14.180
It had a really cool idea where it initially
predicted a coarse result, and then, this

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:20.210
result was matched with the hairstyles found
in public data repositories, and the closest

00:01:20.210 --> 00:01:22.230
match was presented to us.

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:27.920
Clearly, this often meant that we got something
that was similar to the photograph, but often

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:31.110
not exactly the hairstyle we were seeking.

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:36.550
And now, hold on to your papers because this
work introduces a learning-based framework

00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:43.520
that can create a full reconstruction by itself,
without external help, and now, squeeze that

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:47.660
paper, because it works not only only for
images, but for videos too!

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:48.660
Woohoo!

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:54.250
It works for shorter hairstyles, long hair,
and even takes into consideration motion and

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:56.430
external forces as well.

00:01:56.430 --> 00:02:00.680
The heart of the architecture behind this
technique is this pair of neural networks,

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:06.100
where the one above creates the predicted
hair geometry for each frame, while the other

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:11.299
tries to look backwards in the data and try
to predict the appropriate motions that should

00:02:11.299 --> 00:02:12.480
be present.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:17.170
Interestingly, it only needs two consecutive
frames to make these predictions, and adding

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:20.810
more information does not seem to improve
its results.

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:22.760
That is very little data.

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:23.849
Quite remarkable.

00:02:23.849 --> 00:02:29.030
Also, note that there are a lot of moving
parts here in the full paper, for instance,

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:35.019
this motion is first predicted in 2D, and
is then extrapolated to 3D afterwards.

00:02:35.019 --> 00:02:40.909
Let’s have a look at this comparison - indeed,
it seems to produce smoother and more appealing

00:02:40.909 --> 00:02:42.629
results than this older technique.

00:02:42.629 --> 00:02:48.609
But if we look here, this other method seems
even better, so what about that?

00:02:48.609 --> 00:02:53.610
Well, this method had access to multiple views
of the model, which is significantly more

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:59.300
information than what this new technique has
that only needs a simple monocular 2D video

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:02.359
from our phone, or from the internet.

00:03:02.359 --> 00:03:06.670
The fact that they are even comparable is
absolutely amazing.

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:11.620
If you have a look at the paper, you will
see that it even contains a hair growing component

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:13.040
in this architecture.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:18.139
And as you see, the progress in computer graphics
research is also absolutely amazing.

00:03:18.139 --> 00:04:06.930
And we are even being paid 
for

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:08.440
doing this.

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:09.499
Unreal.

00:04:09.499 --> 00:04:16.850
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

